"2007 Proceedings of the Workshop on Algorithm Engineering and Experiments (ALENEX) | Linearization: Locally Self-Stabilizing Sorting in Graphs (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/1.9781611972870.10)\nciteturn9688view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/1.9781611972870.10\",\"lineno\":54}); Total lines: 388\nL0: cite0†Search L1: \nL2: Search\nL3: \nL4:   * cite1†This Book L5:   * cite2†Anywhere L6:   * cite3†Books L7:   * cite4†Journals L8:   * cite5†Proceedings L9: \nL10: Quick Search in Books\nL11: \nL12: Enter Search Terms[Input: Enter Search Terms][Input][Input]Search[Input]\nL13: \nL14: Quick Search anywhere\nL15: \nL16: Enter Search Terms[Input: Enter Search Terms]Search\nL17: \nL18: Quick Search anywhere\nL19: \nL20: Enter Search Terms[Input: Enter Search Terms][Input][Input][Input][Input]Search\nL21: \nL22: Quick Search anywhere\nL23: \nL24: Enter Search Terms[Input: Enter Search Terms][Input][Input]Search\nL25: \nL26: Quick Search anywhere\nL27: Enter Search Terms[Input: Enter Search Terms][Input][Input]Search\nL28: \nL29: cite6†Advanced Search L30: \nL31: cite7†Shopping cart with 0 item L32: \nL33:   * cite8†Register / Sign In L34: \nL35:   * cite9†Access via your Institution L36: \nL37: cite10†Skip main navigation Close Drawer Menu Open Drawer Menu\nL38:   * cite11†Proceedings Series L39:     * cite12†Algorithm Engineering & Experiments (ALENEX) L40:     * cite13†Algorithmic Principles of Computer Systems-APOCS L41:     * cite14†Analytic Algorithmics and Combinatorics (ANALCO) L42:     * cite15†Applied and Computational Discrete Algorithms (ACDA) L43:     * cite16†Combinatorial Scientific Computing (CSC) L44:     * cite17†Control and its Applications L45:     * cite18†Data Mining L46:     * cite19†Discrete Algorithms (SODA) L47:     * cite20†Mathematics for Industry L48:     * cite21†Parallel Processing for Scientific Computing (PP) L49:     * cite22†Simplicity in Algorithms-SOSA L50: cite23†Previous chapter L51: \nL52: cite24†Next chapter L53: \nL54: Proceedings\nL55: \nL56: 2007 Proceedings of the Ninth Workshop on Algorithm Engineering and Experiments (ALENEX)\nL57: # Linearization: Locally Self-Stabilizing Sorting in Graphs\nL58: \nL59: Authors: cite25†Melih Onus , cite26†Andrea Richa , and cite27†Christian Scheideler cite28†Authors Info & Affiliations L60: \nL61: Pages 99 - 108\nL62: \nL63: cite29†https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611972870.10†doi.org L64: \nL65: cite30†Get Access L66: \nL67: cite31†BibTeX L68: \nL69: cite0†Tools L70: ## Abstract\nL71: We consider the problem of designing a distributed algorithm that, given an arbitrary connected graph G of nodes with unique labels, converts G into a sorted list of nodes. This algorithm should be as simple as possible and, for scalability, should guarantee a polylogarithmic runtime as well as at most a polylogarithmic increase in the degree of each node during its execution.\nL72: Furthermore, it should be self-stabilizing, that is, it should be able to eventually construct a sorted list from any state in which the graph is connected. It turns out that satisfying all of these demands at the same time is not easy.\nL73: Our basic approach towards this goal is the so-called linearization technique: each node v repeatedly does the following with its neighbors:\nL74: \nL75: •\nL76: \nL77: for its left (i.e., smaller) neighbors u_{1}, …, u_{k} in the order of decreasing labels, v replaces {v, u_{1}}, …, {v, u_{k}} by {v, u_{1}}, {u_{1},u_{2}},…, {u_{k − 1},u_{k}}, and\nL78: \nL79: •\nL80: for its right (i.e., larger) neighbors w_{1}, …, w_{ℓ} in the order of increasing labels, v replaces {v, w_{1}}, …, {v, w_{ℓ}} by {v, w_{1}}, {w_{1}, w_{2}}, …, {w_{ℓ − 1}, w_{ℓ}}.\nL81: As shown in this paper, this technique transforms any connected graph into a sorted list, but there are graphs for which this can take a long time. Hence, we propose several extensions of the linearization technique and experimentally evaluate their performance. Our results indicate that some of these have a polylogarithmic performance, so there is hope that there are distributed algorithms that can achieve all of our goals above.\nL82: ## Get full access to this chapter\nL83: \nL84: View all available purchase options and get full access to this chapter.\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Get Access L87: \nL88: Already a Subscriber? Sign in as an cite32†individual†sso.siam.org or via your cite33†institution L89: \nL90: ## Information & Authors\nL91: \nL92: ### Information\nL93: #### Published In\nL94: \nL95: cite34†Image: cover image Proceedings L96: \nL97: 2007 Proceedings of the Ninth Workshop on Algorithm Engineering and Experiments (ALENEX)\nL98: \nL99: Pages: 99 - 108\nL100: \nL101: Editors: David Applegate, AT&T Labs-Research, Florham Park, New Jersey and Gerth Stølting Brodal, University of Aarhus, Aarhus, Denmark\nL102: \nL103: DOI: cite29†10.1137/1.9781611972870.10†doi.org L104: \nL105: ISBN (International Standard Book Number) (Online): 978-1-61197-287-0\nL106: \nL107: #### Copyright\nL108: \nL109: © 2007 by the Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics.\nL110: #### History\nL111: \nL112: Published online: 18 December 2013\nL113: \nL114: ### Authors\nL115: \nL116: #### Affiliations\nL117: \nL118: Melih Onus\nL119: \nL120: cite35†View all articles by Melih Onus L121: \nL122: Andrea Richa^{cite36†‡ }\nL123: \nL124: cite37†View all articles by Andrea Richa L125: \nL126: Christian Scheideler\nL127: \nL128: cite38†View all articles by Christian Scheideler L129: \nL130: #### Notes\nL131: \nL132: †\nL133: \nL134: This work was supported in part by NSF CAREER award no. 9985284.\nL135: \nL136: ## Metrics & Citations\nL137: \nL138: ### Metrics\nL139: \nL140: [Input]\nL141: ### Citations\nL142: \nL143: If you have the appropriate software installed, you can downloa