e on Computer Science and Information Technology (CSIT) | 1 Jul 2018\nL189:   * cite49†Autonomous Edge Resource Organization with Smallcell Integration in 5G†doi.org L190: \nL191: 2018 IEEE International Conference on Communications Workshops (ICC Workshops) | 1 May 2018\nL192: \nL193:   * cite50†Self-Stabilizing Supervised Publish-Subscribe Systems†doi.org L194: \nL195: 2018 IEEE International Parallel and Distributed Processing Symposium (IPDPS) | 1 May 2018\nL196: \nL197:   * cite51†A Self-stabilizing Hashed Patricia Trie†doi.org L198: \nL199: Stabilization, Safety, and Security of Distributed Systems | 20 October 2018\nL200:   * cite52†Self-stabilizing Overlays for High-Dimensional Monotonic Searchability†doi.org L201: \nL202: Stabilization, Safety, and Security of Distributed Systems | 20 October 2018\nL203: \nL204:   * cite53†On Underlay-Aware Self-Stabilizing Overlay Networks†doi.org L205: \nL206: Stabilization, Safety, and Security of Distributed Systems | 20 October 2018\nL207: \nL208:   * cite54†A Self-stabilizing General De Bruijn Graph†doi.org L209: \nL210: Stabilization, Safety, and Security of Distributed Systems | 7 October 2017\nL211:   * cite55†Analyzing Communication Overhead in Linearizing Peer to Peer System†doi.org L212: \nL213: 2016 6th International Conference on Information and Communication Technology for The Muslim World (ICT4M) | 1 Nov 2016\nL214: \nL215:   * cite56†Linear Sure Bootstraper: Self-Stabilizing Nodes For Network Construction†doi.org L216: \nL217: 2016 International Conference on Computer and Communication Engineering (ICCCE) | 1 Jul 2016\nL218: \nL219:   * cite57†Self-stabilizing Metric Graphs†doi.org --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSelf-stabilizing ring networks on connected graphs (https://publikationen.bibliothek.kit.edu/1000003169)\nciteturn9688view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://publikationen.bibliothek.kit.edu/1000003169\",\"lineno\":47}); Total lines: 79\nL0: cite0†KIT†kit.edu | cite1†KIT-Bibliothek†www.bibliothek.kit.edu | cite2†Impressum†www.bibliothek.kit.edu | cite3†Datenschutz†www.bibliothek.kit.edu L1: \nL2: cite4†Suche im KITopen-Katalog L3: \nL4: #### cite5†Repository KITopen†www.bibliothek.kit.edu L5: \nL6: # Self-stabilizing ring networks on connected graphs\nL7: \nL8: Cramer, Curt; Fuhrmann, Thomas\nL9: ## Abstract:\nL10: Large networks require scalable routing. Traditionally, protocol\nL11: overhead is\nL12: reduced by introducing a hierarchy. This requires aggregation of\nL13: nearby nodes\nL14: under a common address prefix. In fixed networks, this is achieved\nL15: administratively, whereas in wireless ad-hoc networks, dynamic\nL16: assignments of\nL17: nodes to aggregation units are required.\nL18: As a result of the nodes commonly being assigned a random\nL19: network address, the\nL20: majority of proposed ad-hoc routing protocols discovers routes\nL21: between end\nL22: nodes by flooding, thus limiting the network size. Peer-to-peer\nL23: (P2P) overlay\nL24: networks offer scalable routing solutions by employing\nL25: virtualized address spaces,\nL26: yet assume an underlying routing protocol for end-to-end\nL27: connectivity.\nL28: We investigate a cross-layer approach to P2P routing, where the\nL29: virtual address\nL30: space is implemented with a network-layer routing protocol by\nL31: itself. The Iterative\nL32: Successor Pointer Rewiring Protocol (ISPRP) efficiently\nL33: initializes a ring-structured\nL34: network among nodes having but link-layer connectivity. It is\nL35: fully self-organizing\nL36: and issues only a small per-node amount of messages by keeping\nL37: interactions\nL38: between nodes as local as possible.\nL39: ... mehr\nL40: The main contribution of this paper is a proof that ISPRP is\nL41: self-stabilizing, that is,\nL42: starting from an arbitrary initial state, the protocol lets the\nL43: network converge into\nL44: a correct state within a bounded amount of time.\nL45: cite6†Image: Open Access Logo†dbkit.bibliothek.kit.edu L46: \nL47: cite7†Volltext cite8†§ L48: \nL49: DOI: 10.5445/IR/1000003169\nL50: \nL51: Exportieren als ...\nL52: \nL53: cite9†BibTeX (UTF-8) cite10†BibTeX (ASCII) cite11†EndNote/Refer (.enw) cite12†RIS cite13†CSL-JSON cite14†ISI L54: \nL55: Seitenaufrufe: 211\nL56: seit 25.09.2018\nL57: \nL58: Downloads: 1138\nL59: seit 09.01.2009\nL60: \nL61: cite15†Image: Cover der Publikation†dbkit.bibliothek.kit.edu L62: \nL63: auf\nL64: \nL65: [Input]\nL66: Zugehörige Institution(en) am KIT  | Institut für Telematik (TM)\nL67: Publikationstyp  | Forschungsbericht/Preprint\nL68: Publikationsjahr  | 2005\nL69: Sprache  | Deutsch\nL70: Identifikator  | ISSN: 1432-7864\nL71: urn:nbn:de:swb:90-31697\nL72: KITopen-ID: 1000003169\nL73: Verlag  | Universität Karlsruhe (TH)\nL74: Serie  | Interner Bericht. Fakultät für Informatik, Universität Karlsruhe ; 2005,5\nL75: \nL76: KIT – Die Universität in der Helmholtz-Gemeinschaft\nL77: \nL78: KITopen Landing Page\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\npv4.dvi (https://techrep.csc.ncsu.edu/2005/TR-2005-25.pdf)\nciteturn9688view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://techrep.csc.ncsu.edu/2005/TR-2005-25.pdf\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 675\nL0@P0: Self-Stabilizing Structured Ring Topology P2P Systems\nL1@P0: Ayman Shaker and Douglas S. Reeves\nL2@P0: Departments of Electrical and Computer Engineering and Computer Science\nL3@P0: N. C. State University\nL4@P0: {ashaker,reeves}@ncsu.edu\nL5@P0: Abstract\nL6@P0: We propose a self-stabilizing and modeless peer-to\u0002peer(P2P) network construction and maintenance protocol,\nL7@P0: called the Ring Network(RN) protocol. The RN protocol, when\nL8@P0: started on a network of peers that are in an arbitrary state, will\nL9@P0: cause the network to converge to a structured P2P system with\nL10@P0: a directed ring topology, where peers are ordered according to\nL11@P0: their identifiers. Furthermore, the RN protocol maintains this\nL12@P0: structure in the face of peer joins and departures.\nL13@P0: The RN protocol is a distributed and asynchronous\nL14@P0: message-passing protocol, which fits well the autonomous be\u0002havior of peers in a P2P system. The RN protocol requires\nL15@P0: only the existence of a bootstrapping system which is weakly\nL16@P0: connected. Peers do not need to be informed of any global\nL17@P0: network state, nor do they need to assist in repairing the net\u0002work topology when they leave. We provide a proof of the self\u0002stabilizing nature of the protocol, and experimentally measure\nL18@P0: the average cost (in time and number of messages) to achieve\nL19@P0: convergence.\nL20@P0: 1 Introduction\nL21@P0: P2P systems are decentralized and have several attrac\u0002tive properties like fault tolerance, load-balancing and self\u0002scaling[3]. P2P systems construct and maintain overlay net\u0002works with different network topologies, depending on appli\u0002cation requirements. Structured P2P systems impose specific\nL22@P0: local relationships between the peers, which gives rise to a\nL23@P0: global structure. The advantage of this structure is that it can\nL24@P0: then be used by the P2P system for efficient data placement,\nL25@P0: search, and retrieval.\nL26@P0: Current protocols for the construction and maintenance of\nL27@P0: specific structures generally rely on simplifying assumptions\nL28@P0: that are either undesirable or unrealistic. First, most such sys\u0002tems require or assume a specific initial configuration of the\nL29@P0: peers. Second, the construction and maintenance protocols as\u0002sume that the network remains in an ideal or close to ideal\nL30@P0: topology at all times. If the topology of the network deviates\nL31@P0: from this ideal, then these construction and maintenance pro\u0002tocols may cease to function correctly. Third, a dynamic net\u0002work, in the sense that peers arrive and depart continuously,\nL32@P0: can become partitioned. If this happens, the construction and\nL33@P0: maintenance protocols generally have no way to bring the net\u0002work back to the desired topology. One result of these limita\u0002tions is the difficulty of merging two structured P2P networks\nL34@P0: efficiently and smoothly. Existing construction and mainte\u0002nance protocols generally do not allow such merging, even if\nL35@P0: each network individually has the desired structure.\nL36@P0: In this paper we propose the Ring Network(RN) protocol;\nL37@P0: a distributed construction and maintenance protocol that gives\nL38@P0: rise to a sorted Ring Network, starting from an arbitrary topol\u0002ogy. This protocol is able to correctly merge two P2P networks\nL39@P0: into a single ring, or fix a network that is somehow partitioned.\nL40@P0: Furthermore, the protocol does not require the manual creation\nL41@P0: of a “seed” network, unlike most structured P2P systems. The\nL42@P0: protocol also does not require peers to be informed of or mon\u0002itor the global state of the network, before deciding what up\u0002dates to perform. Each peer simply runs the proposed con\u0002struction and maintenance protocols, without specific knowl\u0002edge of the current state of the network topology, and indepen\u0002dently of other peers.\nL43@P0: Existing methods of constructing structured P2P overlay\nL44@P0: networks generally adopt a top-down view of topology con\u0002struction and maintenance. These methods may require cen\u0002tral coordination, may have difficulty keeping up with the rate\nL45@P0: of change of typical P2P systems, and/or may not tolerate or\nL46@P0: recover from errors or failures. Efforts to contain or eliminate\nL47@P0: these problems sometimes lead to complex protocol “fixes”.\nL48@P0: Our method, in contrast, may be thought of as a bottom-up\nL49@P0: approach to constructing structured networks, in a way that is\nL50@P0: simple, robust, and fully distributed.\nL51@P0: The next section presents the network model and a formal\nL52@P0: definition of the problem. Section 3 is a review of related work.\nL53@P0: Section 4 describes the proposed protocol. Section 5 evaluates\nL54@P0: the correctness and the performance of the protocol. The paper\nL55@P0: is concluded in section 6.\nL56@P0: 2 Problem Statement\nL57@P0: A P2P network is a logical overlay network formed on top\nL58@P0-1: of a fixed infrastructure network, such as the Internet. There\u0002fore, any peer u in the network can communicate or send amessage to any other peer v directly, if u knows the network\nL59@P1: address of v. In such a case, v is said to be a neighbor of u.\nL60@P1: A P2P network topology is defined by the set of peers in a\nL61@P1: network, and the neighbor relationships between those peers.\nL62@P1: This topology can be represented by a graph, with vertexes\nL63@P1: representing peers, and a directed edge from vertex u to vertex\nL64@P1: v if v is a neighbor of u. Let the neighbors of peer u be denoted\nL65@P1: as u.Γ. 1 We further denote the ith neighbor of peer u by u.Γi.\nL66@P1: The 0th neighbor, u.Γ0, is a special neighbor of u and is also\nL67@P1: called the successor of u. If v is the successor of u, then u is a\nL68@P1: predecessor of v. If v is the ith neighbor of u, v = u.Γi, then\nL69@P1: let u.index(v)= i.\nL70@P1: Each peer has a unique identifier or ID, in the form of a\nL71@P1: nonnegative integer. Let the largest possible ID assignable to\nL72@P1: a peer be m − 1. We assume each peer knows the value of m.\nL73@P1: The distance from a peer u to a peer v is defined by a distance\nL74@P1: function, dm(u, v)= v − u, where subtraction is performed\nL75@P1: modulo m.\nL76@P1: 2 For simplicity of presentation, we omit the sub\u0002script m from the distance function in this paper. The smaller\nL77@P1: the value of d(u, v) the closer v is said to be to u. For the\nL78@P1: purposes of this paper we define a P2P network as being a ring\nL79@P1: network if for each peer u in the network, u’s successor, v, is\nL80@P1: the peer closest to u from the set of all peers and v \u0002= u (unless\nL81@P1: the system consists of a single peer, in which case the succes\u0002sor of u will be itself). Note that the ring network is directed.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2504.02448v1] Towards Learning-Augmented Peer-to-Peer Networks: Self-Stabilizing Graph Linearization with Untrusted Advice (https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.02448v1)\nciteturn9688view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.02448v1\",\"lineno\":45}); Total lines: 160\nL9: # Title:Towards Learning-Augmented Peer-to-Peer Networks: Self-Stabilizing Graph Linearization with Untrusted Advice\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Vijeth Aradhya , cite6†Christian Scheideler L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Towards Learning-Augmented Peer-to-Peer Networks: Self-Stabilizing Graph Linearization with Untrusted Advice, by Vijeth Aradhya and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Distributed peer-to-peer systems are widely popular due to their decentralized nature, which ensures that no peer is critical for the functionality of the system. However, fully decentralized solutions are usually much harder to design, and tend to have a much higher overhead compared to centralized approaches, where the peers are connected to a powerful server. On the other hand, centralized approaches have a single point of failure.\nL17: Thus, is there some way to combine their advantages without inheriting their disadvantages? To that end, we consider a supervised peer-to-peer approach where the peers can ask a potentially unreliable supervisor for advice. This is in line with the increasingly popular algorithmic paradigm called algorithms with predictions or learning-augmented algorithms, but we are the first to consider it in the context of peer-to-peer networks.\nL18: > Specifically, we design self-stabilizing algorithms for the fundamental problem of distributed graph linearization, where peers are supposed to recover the \"sorted line\" network from any initial network after a transient fault. With the help of the supervisor, peers can recover the sorted line network in $O(\\log n)$ time, if the advice is correct; otherwise, the algorithm retains its original recovery time (i.e., without any supervisor).\nL19: A crucial challenge that we overcome is to correctly compose multiple self-stabilizing algorithms, that is, one that processes and exploits the advice, and another that does not rely on the advice at all. Our key technical contributions combine ideas from the fields of overlay networks and proof-labeling schemes. Finally, we give a matching lower bound of $\\Omega(\\log n)$ for the recovery time of any algorithm if the advice can be corrupted, where $n$ is the network size.\nL20: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2504.02448 [cs.DC]\nL22:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2504.02448v1 [cs.DC] for this version)\nL23:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.02448†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Vijeth Aradhya [cite12†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Thu, 3 Apr 2025 10:07:41 UTC (385 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Towards Learning-Augmented Peer-to-Peer Networks: Self-Stabilizing Graph Linearization with Untrusted Advice, by Vijeth Aradhya and 1 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite7†View PDF L36:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite13†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite14†